findings.claim:
Order in the BSB copy:
(1) title; (2) dedication to Ulrich Fugger, pp. 3-16;
(3) Latin translation, pp. 17-117, headed 'Doctissimi Pselli perspicuus liber ... Gulielmo Xylandro Augustano interprete';
(4) 'Guilielmi Xylandri Augustani, in Pselli opusculum, à se conversum, Annotationes quaedam', pp. 118-163, ending 'FINIS. Deo soli gloria.';
(5) Greek text, new pagination pp. 1-103, with no separate Greek title page or preface, headed 'ΤΟΥ ΣΟΦΩΤΑΤΟΥ ΨΕΛΛΟΥ ΕΥΣΥΝΟΠΤΟΝ ΣΥΝΤΑΓΜΑ ...'; music begins p. 23, geometry p. 34, astronomy p. 76;
(6) Carmen de Philosophia, pp. 104-152;
(7) Zonaras poems (Greek, then Latin 'Eiusdem'), pp. 153-161;
(8) Naenia for Betuleius, pp. 162-175.

Greek arithmetic (pp. 1-22) has 11 marginal notes on 9 pages:
p. 2 'πολλαπλασιάσειεν' (text '*πολλαπλάσειεν');
p. 5 '*εἰ.';
p. 6 '*μείζονες' and '*ἥττονες' (text has them swapped);
p. 10 'Forte scripserat ἢ καὶ';
p. 12 'Vide Annotationes, ubi docetur, hic & alibi legendum διὰ πασῶν καὶ διὰ τεσσάρων, & διὰ πασῶν καὶ διὰ πέντε';
p. 15 '*ἑκάστην(?)';
p. 16 '*προσθήκη' and 'Lege ἐπισύνθεσιν, uno vocabulo';
p. 20 '*ἐγγώνια';
p. 21 '*πρίσμασι'.

Wherever I compared, Xylander's printed Greek keeps the readings of the Paris 1545 reprint that he corrects in his margin:
- 1545 f. 5v 'οὓς ἂν τῶν ἄλλων πολλαπλάσειεν' = Xylander p. 2, margin 'πολλαπλασιάσειεν';
- 1545 f. 8v 'Ἀτελεῖς δέ, οἱ τῶν ἑαυτῶν μερῶν ἥττονες ... ὑπερτελεῖς δέ, οἱ μείζονες' = Xylander p. 6, margin swaps them;
- 1545 f. 19r 'τοῖς πράγμασι πρὸς τὰ γωνιώδη ἐπίπεδα' = Xylander's 'πράγμασι', margin '*πρίσμασι'.
This fits his having set the Greek from the printed edition (1532 or its 1545 reprint) and corrected it only in the margin, but that is my inference. The 1532 edition itself was not consulted.

Harles adds these editions:
- Latin 'Arithmeticae, Musicae et Geometriae compendia, Guil. Xylandro interprete', Heidelberg, Abr. Smesmann 1591, 8°, 'sine praefat. et adnotatt.', with part of an oration by C. Peucer;
- Latin Xylander, Leiden 1647;
- citations of a Greek-Latin Leipzig 1590, a Wittenberg 1560 and an 'Aug. Vindel. 1554' edition, which look like ghosts or confusions;
- Paris 1532 [Venice] via the Pinelli catalogue;
- footnote i: Lambert Alard printed Psellus's Music with his own Latin version in De veterum Musica (Schleusingen 1636).

